```json
[
  {
    "stratum": "Strategy / Commitment",
    "text": "My wife and I own 140 acres that has been in her family for three generations, currently leased to a soybean farmer for $18,000/year. A developer has offered $2.1 million for 60 of those acres for a housing subdivision, closing within four months if we accept. Separately, a farm-to-table consultant has proposed we convert the whole property into an agritourism operation — cabins, a market, seasonal events — estimating $400,000 in upfront costs (which we'd need to borrow) and a break-even around year four, with no guaranteed ceiling on income after that. We can't do both: the subdivision would fragment the land in a way that makes agritourism impossible later, and if we start borrowing for the agritourism build-out we won't qualify for additional financing if that stalls. My father-in-law wants the land kept whole; my wife is more open to selling part of it if it secures our retirement. We have to give the developer an answer in three weeks. Which direction should we commit to?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Strategy / Commitment",
    "text": "I run a 22-person software company that makes scheduling tools for small clinics. We currently have a web app that works fine on tablets but is clunky on phones, and support tickets increasingly ask for a 'real' mobile app. Building native iOS and Android apps properly would take our two senior engineers off all other work for an estimated seven months and cost around $310,000 in salary time, after which we'd have three codebases to maintain instead of one. The alternative is to invest three months and about $90,000 rebuilding the web app as a proper mobile-responsive progressive web app, which covers most of the same functionality but can't do push notifications or offline charting, both of which two of our largest clients have specifically asked for. We can't pursue both paths at once given our engineering headcount, and reversing course after starting the native build would mean writing off most of the sunk work. Our board wants a decision at the next meeting in three weeks. Which direction should we commit to?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Operations / Execution",
    "text": "Our 300-bed hospital has committed to replacing its legacy patient records system with a new EHR platform across all departments — that decision was finalized by the board two months ago and isn't being revisited. The vendor can onboard one department every three weeks, and we have twelve departments total. Emergency and ICU have the highest patient-safety stakes if something goes wrong during transition, but they also have the least staff slack to absorb training time. Outpatient clinics have more scheduling flexibility but their billing systems are tightly integrated with three other departments, so sequencing them wrong could create billing gaps. We have a hard deadline: the legacy system's support contract expires in nine months, and the vendor won't compress the three-week-per-department pace. Staff can only be pulled from clinical duty for training in half-day blocks, twice per department cycle. Given these constraints, how should we sequence the twelve departments and structure the training blocks so we hit the nine-month deadline without leaving any department under-supported during its own go-live week?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Operations / Execution",
    "text": "We own a five-location fast-casual chain and have already decided to extend delivery service from our current 11am–8pm window to 11am–11pm at all locations starting next month — this was set at the owners' meeting and isn't up for debate again. Each location currently has one driver on shift during peak dinner hours and none after 8pm. Kitchen staff finish prep and close down at 8:30pm currently. To support the extended window we can either hire two additional part-time drivers per location (10 total, at roughly $16/hour) or convert to a shared driver pool dispatched from our two busiest locations to cover the three smaller ones, which is cheaper but adds 8–12 minutes to delivery times for the smaller stores. Kitchen staff would need their shifts extended by three hours, which our current staff have mixed willingness to do — two of five locations already have a shortage of closing-shift volunteers. We need the new hours live in five weeks with no drop in delivery times below our current 30-minute average. How should we phase in staffing and driver coverage across the five locations to hit that deadline?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Brand / Creative",
    "text": "My craft brewery, founded twelve years ago on a scrappy, punk-rock identity — hand-drawn labels, swear words in beer names, a slogan of 'brewed by weirdos for weirdos' — was acquired eight months ago by a mid-size beverage company that wants to triple our distribution into grocery chains next year. Grocery buyers have told our new parent company that the current labels and names won't pass their family-friendly shelf review, and three of our best-selling beers would need renaming. Our original core customers, mostly regulars at our two taprooms, treat the irreverent identity as the entire reason they're loyal to us, and some have already started grumbling online about 'selling out' since the acquisition news broke. The parent company isn't asking us to abandon the brand, just to make it retailer-safe, and they're leaving the actual creative direction to us. I need to figure out how the brand should present itself now — voice, naming approach, visual identity — in a way that can live on grocery shelves without gutting what our existing customers feel ownership over. What should that identity be?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Brand / Creative",
    "text": "I direct a mid-sized environmental nonprofit that just merged with a smaller wildlife rehabilitation group after both organizations struggled separately with donor fatigue. The wildlife group had a beloved, folksy identity — a hand-drawn fox logo, first-name-basis newsletters, a name locals recognized from 30 years of school visits. My organization has a more clinical, policy-focused identity aimed at grant committees and legislative staff, with a name full of acronyms nobody outside the field recognizes. The merger agreement requires we operate under one unified name and visual identity within six months to qualify for a combined federal grant, but neither board will agree to simply drop into the other's identity — the wildlife group's donors are almost entirely small individual givers who respond to warmth, while our donors are foundations that respond to institutional credibility. We have a joint board meeting in five weeks where I need to present a proposed direction for the combined name and voice. What should that identity be?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Evidence Interpretation",
    "text": "Our regional grocery chain rolled out a loyalty app eighteen months ago, and average weekly spend per loyalty member is up 11% compared to the eighteen months prior, which our marketing team is citing as proof the app drove increased spending and wants funded for expansion to two more states. But looking at the same period, we also raised prices roughly 6% chain-wide due to supplier costs, and we closed our two lowest-performing stores, which pulled down our low-spend customer average in the earlier baseline. Loyalty members also skew toward customers who were already frequent shoppers before the app existed — sign-up required an existing account with six months of purchase history, which excludes new or infrequent shoppers by design. I have the month-by-month spend data for loyalty members, non-loyalty members, and the two closed stores' historical averages, all available. Marketing is asking for a $2 million budget commitment next quarter based on this 11% figure. Before that conversation, what does this data actually show about whether the app increased spending?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Evidence Interpretation",
    "text": "Our school district piloted a mandatory after-school tutoring block for struggling 8th-grade math students last year, and standardized test scores for that cohort rose from an average of 62% to 71% proficiency, a bigger jump than any grade level had shown in the past five years. The superintendent wants to cite this as evidence the tutoring program works and expand it district-wide next year at a cost of roughly $1.2 million. However, the same year we also switched to a new math textbook series district-wide for all grades, not just the tutoring cohort, and state testing standards were revised to a slightly easier passing threshold that year — a change that added roughly 3–4 points to every grade's proficiency rate district-wide, based on the state's own equating report. I have the proficiency data broken out by grade level, including grades that didn't receive tutoring, for the past three years. Before we present this to the school board as justification for the expansion, what does this data actually show about whether the tutoring program caused the improvement?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Product / Service Design",
    "text": "I own a mid-size gym that currently has one flat membership price of $45/month with full access to everything. We're seeing two problems: price-sensitive members drop off in January after New Year signups, and our highest-usage members — the ones who come with a personal trainer or use the pool and classes daily — feel like they're getting the same deal as someone who visits twice a month, which has led a few to ask about better perks for their loyalty. I want to introduce tiered membership but I'm not sure how many tiers make sense, what should be gated behind higher tiers (classes, pool access, guest passes, trainer discounts), or whether a lower-priced limited tier would cannibalize existing full-price members rather than attracting new price-sensitive ones. We have the facility capacity to support more visits than we currently get, so usage isn't the constraint — the goal is retention and matching price to perceived value. What should the tier structure look like, and what should be included at each level?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Product / Service Design",
    "text": "Our regional bank wants to launch a checking account product aimed specifically at college students, a segment we currently have almost no presence in. We know from competitor research that students care about no monthly fees, easy peer-to-peer transfers, and some kind of overdraft forgiveness, since many are managing money independently for the first time and irregular income from part-time jobs. We also know from our own data that this segment is expensive to acquire but has high lifetime value if they stay with us past graduation, when their income and product needs grow. We're not deciding whether to enter this segment — that's already approved and budgeted — but we haven't settled what the account itself should actually include: what fee waivers, what overdraft structure, whether to bundle a secured credit-builder card, what if any savings component to attach, and how to structure any graduation-triggered upgrade to a standard account. What should this account's features and structure look like?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Finance / Resource Allocation",
    "text": "Our small city has a one-time $4 million infrastructure grant from the state, and there are three projects competing for it: repaving Main Street, which has been rated in 'poor' condition by the state's own road audit and affects the highest-traffic commercial corridor; replacing the 60-year-old water main under the east side neighborhood, which has had four breaks in the last two years and serves about 1,800 households; and building a new branch library in the fast-growing south side, where the current nearest library is 20 minutes away and the city council has fielded resident requests for years. Each project has been independently estimated at roughly $2.5–3 million if done at full scope, meaning the grant can't fully fund more than one, though each could be scaled down to fit alongside partial funding of another. The grant must be allocated by the end of this fiscal year or it reverts to the state. How should we divide the $4 million across these three claims?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Finance / Resource Allocation",
    "text": "My startup just closed a funding round giving us 18 months of runway, and I need to set next quarter's headcount budget across three teams that are all asking for more people: engineering wants two more hires to finish a delayed integration our biggest customer is waiting on, sales wants one more account executive because our current two are both over capacity and we're losing deals to slow follow-up, and customer support wants one more hire because response times have slipped past our SLA on two of the last three months. I have budget approved for exactly two new hires this quarter, not four, and each team lead believes their need is the most urgent one blocking company growth right now. Engineering's delayed integration is tied to a renewal worth $180,000 annually; the sales bottleneck is estimated to be costing us two to three lost deals a month at an average deal size of $40,000; and the support SLA slippage hasn't yet caused any cancellations but is showing up in customer satisfaction scores. How should I allocate these two headcount slots across the three teams?"
  }
]
```
